[" Use", -0.00040282969712279737], [" Intel", -8.677825927734375]]}, {"t": " Intel", "lp": -0.0003567378153093159, "top": [[" Intel", -0.0003567378153093159], ["Intel", -8.347002983093262]]}, {"t": "'s", "lp": -0.00019999357755295932, "top": [["'s", -0.00019999357755295932], ["\u7684", -9.0259428024292]]}, {"t": " **", "lp": -0.0070727854035794735, "top": [[" **", -0.0070727854035794735], [" **+", -5.66909646987915]]}, {"t": "C", "lp": -0.00013125804252922535, "top": [["C", -0.00013125804252922535], ["c", -9.272672653198242]]}, {"t": "++", "lp": -0.16242752969264984, "top": [["++", -0.16242752969264984], ["XX", -1.8997799158096313]]}, {"t": "**", "lp": -0.024239124730229378, "top": [["**", -0.024239124730229378], ["/", -3.9857821464538574]]}], "text": "# PKGBUILD build() Function: Step-by-Step Explanation\n\nLet me break down this `build()` function line by line.\n\n## The Setup\n\n```bash\nsource /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh --force\n```\nThis loads the Intel oneAPI environment variables (compiler paths, library paths, etc.). The `--force` flag forces the environment to be set up even if it appears already configured. This is essential because we're using Intel's `icx`/`icpx` compilers, which aren't on the default PATH.\n\n```bash\nset -e\n```\nThis makes the script exit immediately if any command returns a non-zero status (an error). A safety net so a failed command doesn't cascade into broken output.\n\n## The CMake Invocation\n\nNow the main `cmake` command. Let me flag each part:\n\n| Flag | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| `-S \"${srcdir}/llama.cpp\"` | **S**ource directory \u2014 where the source code lives |\n| `-B \"${srcdir}/build\"` | **B**uild directory \u2014 where build artifacts go |\n| `-G Ninja` | Use the **Ninja** build generator (fast, minimal output) |\n| `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` | Optimize build with `-O3`-style flags, strip debug info |\n| `-DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr` | Where `make install` will place files (system root) |\n| `-DCMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR=lib` | Put libraries in `/usr/lib` (not `/usr/lib64`) |\n| `-DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=icx` | Use Intel's **C** compiler (`icx`) |\n| `-DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=icpx` | Use Intel's **C++**"}